# AD107 RTX 4060 Laptop (10de:28e0, G614JV) --fuserows physical fuse-row capture, 2026-11-22
# ssdiag 0.8.7 guard-fixed build (CRC 0x4555 / FUSEDEBUG 0x400000 Ada accepted).
# IFF_RECORD 0x033f02ff: START=767, LAST=831 (832 rows) - matches the family
# prediction shared with rison1337's desktop 4060 (10de:2882). Two passes
# match (832 rows), address restored (FUSEADDR saved 831 == LAST).
# Provenance: user paste (Windows, elevated probe-driver run).
# CLASSIFICATION (revised 2026-11-23, security-review re-check): these rows
# carry unknown per-device fuse material that is ADDITIONALLY ENCRYPTED -
# i.e. even if the plaintext contains key material, what the controller
# exposes is ciphertext, unusable without keys we do not have and cannot
# extract. Sharing the FULL row dump is therefore cleared as SAFE by the
# team's security review (supersedes the 11-22 caution notice). Original
# observation stands: entropy near sample max, no decodable structure,
# sizes match no known public format (HDCP 1.x 280B / HDCP 2.2 RSA blocks
# were hypothesized by the security review and found structurally absent).

# Rows 0x2e..0x57, 0x6a..0x80, 0x85..0x88, 0x8e..0xa8, 0xac..0xae, 0xb7..0xbc are device-unique random material (byte entropy 89-100% of
# sample max after dedup; no decodable structure; sizes do NOT match any
# known public format incl. HDCP 1.x 280B / HDCP 2.2 RSA blocks, and differ
# per die). Entropy cannot distinguish secret keys from public verification
# material - both are random-looking by design - so the classification stays
# OPEN until an NVIDIA-side decode exists; HDCP was hypothesized by our
# team’s security review but has no
# structural evidence. Anyone providing row dumps is informed of this
# classification in advance; sharing is optional, and these ranges may be
# redacted or withheld without losing analysis value (the fuse-pool records
# are the analysis-relevant part).
